  "body": "Clicking TOP/BOT on the view cube threw the roll away. A face click commands `Camera::toward(face normal)`; at a Z pole the normalized direction is exactly `[0, 0, +-1]`, so `toward` read `yaw = atan2(0, 0) = 0` and `basis_right`'s zero-guard returned literal world `+X` whatever the yaw - every pole click landed on one canonical orientation and the model spun up to 180 degrees on screen (#622 repro: orbit near-overhead at yaw ~90 deg, click TOP, the part rotates half a turn).\n\n## The change\n\n`Camera::toward` (`viewport.rs`) grows one clause: when the normalized direction is exactly a Z pole (`n[2].abs() == 1.0`), the yaw is the *pre-click* camera's yaw snapped to the nearest quarter turn, `(yaw / FRAC_PI_2).round() * FRAC_PI_2`, instead of the vanishing atan2. `basis_right`'s pole guard evaluates that yaw - `(-yaw.sin(), yaw.cos(), 0.0)` - which is the same expression the guard already produces for every non-pole view, extended to the one point where `cross(forward, +Z)` underflows `normalize`'s EPSILON floor (`cos(pi/2)^2 ~ 3.7e-33`).\n\nResult: a near-pole click turns the screen frame at most 45 degrees from the up it had (the snap's own bound); screen up/right still ride world axes at the pole (the surviving half of the old law); views sharing a quarter turn roll alike; and a second click at the same pole is exact idempotence - the reported double-click twitch cannot happen. Non-pole directions (edges, corners, `oriented`, orbit) are untouched. Rationale in `docs/decisions/622-viewcube-roll-snap.md`.\n\n## Pins (red on main, green with the fix)\n\n- `law_tests::view_cube_tests::pole_views_hold_a_stable_roll` - re-pinned from \"screen right is +X at both poles for arbitrary yaws\" to the quarter-turn law: right `(-sin y_s, cos y_s, 0)`, up `(-cos y_s, -sin y_s, 0)` at TOP / `(cos y_s, sin y_s, 0)` at BOT.\n- `law_tests::view_cube_tests::toward_turns_the_eye_without_moving_it` - new cases: start yaw 2.0 -> TOP lands `pi/2`; start yaw -2.3 -> BOT lands `-pi/2`.\n- `law_tests::view_cube_tests::a_pole_click_keeps_the_roll_the_view_had` - near-pole view (yaw 1.0, pitch 1.5), TOP click lands the on-screen up within 45 degrees of the pre-click up. On main the swing is ~147 degrees.\n- `law_tests::view_cube_tests::a_second_pole_click_lands_the_same_view` - the reported double-click: the second `toward([0,0,1])` changes nothing (passes before and after; it pins the property the issue asked for).\n\n## Tested\n\n- Red first: with only the pins committed, `cargo test -p camcraft-ui --lib view_cube_tests` runs 3 failed / 4 passed, exactly the predicted law breaks - `a pole click keeps the pre-click yaw's quarter turn: 0` (`[-0.5389488413540798, -0.8393630887186533, 0.07073720166770292] -> [-0.0, 1.0, 0.0]`), and `screen right at the [0.0, 0.0, 1.0] pole: [1.0, 0.0, 0.0]`.\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --no-run` builds clean.\n- Green: `cargo test -p camcraft-ui --lib view_cube_tests` - 7 passed, 0 failed.\n- Full lib: `cargo test -p camcraft-ui --lib` - 379 passed, 0 failed (5 ignored). One earlier run failed only on `structural::shell_structure_light`, a theme-sync test this change never touches; it passes in isolation and on re-run - pre-existing parallel global-theme-state flakiness.\n- The `egui_app` view-cube gesture tests (face click orients, arms nothing) pass untouched.\n- Not exercised: the full app suite beyond the two crates, and the on-screen cube itself - the law is pinned instead.\n\nChangelog: view-cube TOP/BOT clicks now land the pole view at the nearest quarter-turn roll instead of spinning the model to canonical +X (#622)\n\nFixes #622\n",
